<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bi Sheng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1051">1051</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bi Sheng was a Chinese artisan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> credited with inventing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> using ceramic characters around 1040 CE. His method is recorded in the writings of the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a>. The invention predated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg">Gutenberg</a>'s press by about four centuries and is counted among the major technological achievements of Song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bi-sheng.jpg" alt="Bi Sheng" width="278" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Popolon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beijing.Musee_imprimerie.caracteres_mobiles.Bisheng.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bi Sheng was an artisan and engineer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> (960–1279). Because he was a commoner and not an educated man, little is known about his life apart from his invention. His gravestone was located in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> province in 1990, and a park and museum opened on the site in April 2023. A subdistrict of Wenquan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huanggang">Huanggang</a>, in Hubei is named after him, as is a crater on the far side of the Moon, named after him on 2 August 2010.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His invention is recorded only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dream-pool-essays">Dream Pool Essays</a> of the scholar-official and polymath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a> (1031–1095), who places it in the Qingli reign. Bi Sheng cut characters in sticky clay, each as thin as the edge of a coin and each forming a single type, and baked them hard. He laid them in an iron frame on an iron plate coated with a mixture of pine resin, wax and paper ashes, warmed the plate until the paste softened, and pressed the type level with a smooth board.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a> notes that the method was neither simple nor easy for two or three copies but extremely quick for hundreds or thousands. Two formes were kept going, so that one could be set while the other was printed. Common characters had twenty or more types each. Types were stored with paper labels, one for each rhyme group, in wooden cases. Bi Sheng also tried wooden type, but it was abandoned because of the grain of the wood and its unevenness after soaking in ink.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ceramic type may have spread after his death to the Tangut kingdom of Western Xia, where a Buddhist text, the Vimalakirti Nirdesa Sutra, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, shows traits of clay type. The method was also passed to his descendants, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>'s councillor Yao Shu had his pupil Yang Gu print language primers with it. The official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-zhen-eunuch">Wang Zhen</a>, active between 1290 and 1333, improved on clay with wooden type. A wealthy printer, Hua Sui, developed bronze type by 1490. Wooden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a> became widespread under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> but did not replace woodblock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, probably because of the expense of making so many pieces or the low cost of a copyist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bi Sheng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bi Sheng</b> invented movable type using ceramic characters around 1040, some four centuries before Gutenberg's printing press.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tangut script, attributed to Yeli Renrong and based on Chinese and Khitan, was used from 1036 to translate and print Chinese and Tibetan works, and Western Xia pioneered movable type after <b>Bi Sheng</b> (972–1051): the Auspicious Tantra of All-Reaching Union is the earliest surviving wooden-type book.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…names are a king under whom a thing was made and an artisan whose method a scholar wrote down: Hangul was created in 1443 under Sejong the Great of Joseon, and the movable type that <b>Bi Sheng</b> made about 1040 is recorded in the writings of Shen Kuo.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-invented-it-first">Who Invented It First?</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1040 the artisan <b>Bi Sheng</b> invented printing with movable clay type, as the scholar Shen Kuo recorded in his Dream Pool Essays.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yingshan County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.74,115.67&amp;year=1041" aria-label="Yingshan County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7545 1043 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bi Sheng"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7995 1313h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7995 1313h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.74,115.67&amp;year=1041">What was here in 1041? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
